## Claim
Text: "They [the Democrats] want to ... ban fracking."
Claim author: Mike Pence
Claim date: October 07, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### 1. On what specific occasion and in what full context did Mike Pence make the statement on October 7, 2020, claiming that Democrats want to ban fracking?
Answer: Mike Pence made the statement during the vice-presidential debate on the evening of Wednesday, October 7, 2020, claiming that "Joe Biden and Kamala Harris... want to abolish fossil fuels and ban fracking which would cost hundreds of thousands of American jobs all across the heartland."

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291

### 2. What was the official policy platform of the Democratic Party regarding fracking as adopted at the 2020 Democratic National Convention?
Answer: The official 2020 Democratic Party platform, as adopted at the convention, "left off a fracking prohibition" and did not include a ban.

Source URL: https://www.spglobal.com/commodityinsights/en/market-insights/latest-news/natural-gas/082520-republicans-warn-of-fracking-ban-oil-and-gas-jobs-loss-on-day-one-of-convention

### 3. Did the 2020 Democratic presidential nominee, Joe Biden, explicitly state a policy goal of implementing a nationwide ban on all hydraulic fracturing?
Answer: No, the 2020 Democratic presidential nominee Joe Biden did not explicitly state a policy goal of a nationwide ban on all hydraulic fracturing; in fact, he repeatedly and explicitly denied it, stating "I am not banning fracking" and "fracking is not going to be on the chopping block."

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291

### 4. Did any proposed federal legislation introduced by Democratic members of Congress in the year 2020 call for a complete and permanent ban on hydraulic fracturing for oil and gas?
Answer: Yes, in 2020 Democratic members of Congress introduced federal legislation that called for a complete nationwide ban on hydraulic fracturing. Specifically, Representatives Darren Soto and Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez introduced the Ban Fracking Act (H.R. 5857), a bill to "phase out fracking nationwide" which would "ban fracking nationwide" starting in 2025, after initial restrictions on new permits and near homes/schools.

Source URL: http://soto.house.gov/media/press-releases/soto-ocasio-cortez-sanders-merkley-unveil-bill-ban-fracking-nationwide

### 5. What were the specific words used by Democratic leaders like Joe Biden, Kamala Harris, or the Democratic National Committee Chair in 2020 when describing their policy on fracking and oil/gas extraction?
Answer: According to the search result, Joe Biden stated specifically: “I am not banning fracking. Let me say that again: I am not banning fracking. No matter how many times Donald Trump lies about me.”

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/08/31/politics/joe-biden-not-banning-fracking/index.html

### 6. Did Joe Biden or the Democratic Party platform distinguish between banning fracking on federal lands versus banning it on all private and state lands?
Answer: Yes, Joe Biden explicitly distinguished between the two, stating he would ban new oil and gas drilling on federal lands while opposing a nationwide ban on fracking. As reported, Biden said at a 2019 town hall that "he does support stopping all 'oil drilling or gas drilling on federal lands,'" but overall he asserted, "I am not banning fracking."

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/08/31/politics/joe-biden-not-banning-fracking/index.html

### 7. Were there any major Democratic Party figures in 2020 who publicly and repeatedly advocated for a total, nationwide ban on fracking as a top policy priority?
Answer: Yes, according to the NPR article, there were major Democratic figures who publicly advocated for a total fracking ban during the 2020 primary season. Specifically, **Elizabeth Warren** tweeted she would "ban fracking—everywhere," and **Bernie Sanders** had talked about banning fracking since his 2016 run, making it part of his broader Green New Deal platform. Both were prominent presidential primary candidates. However, it is important to note that these figures were primary contenders and not the party's eventual nominee; the article also highlights that some Democrats worried these positions could alienate moderate and swing voters, suggesting the ban-fracking stance was not universally embraced across the party.

Source URL: https://www.npr.org/2020/02/11/804487306/proposals-to-ban-fracking-could-hurt-democrats-in-key-states

### 8. How did fact-checking organizations (e.g., PolitiFact, FactCheck.org, Washington Post Fact Checker) assess the specific claim that Joe Biden's 2020 platform called for a total ban on fracking?
Answer: Fact-checking organizations assessed the claim that Joe Biden's 2020 platform called for a total ban on fracking as **"Mostly false."** The evaluation noted that while Biden had made confusing past statements about eliminating or banning fracking, he and his campaign repeatedly clarified during the 2020 race that he did not support a nationwide fracking ban, and his official platform did not include one.

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291

### 9. What is the historical position of the Democratic Party's national platform on fossil fuel extraction and hydraulic fracturing in the cycles preceding 2020?
Answer: Historically, before the 2020 cycle, the Democratic Party's national platform and mainstream figures generally favored regulating, not banning, fossil fuel extraction and fracking. As noted in the source, under President Barack Obama, "his administration focused on tighter regulation of fracking on federal land but largely steered clear of the state-level battles over whether the practice was dangerous, dirty or accelerating climate change."

Source URL: https://www.denverpost.com/2019/09/10/democrats-fracking-bans-politics/

### 10. Did the context of Mike Pence's October 7, 2020, statement involve a debate or discussion where the opposing side clarified their actual policy stance on fracking?
Answer: Yes, the context was the vice-presidential debate on October 7, 2020, and the opposing side explicitly clarified their stance. As noted in the search result, Kamala Harris directly rebutted Pence's claim during the debate, stating that "the American people know that Joe Biden will not ban fracking."

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291

## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals that while some prominent Democratic primary candidates like Elizabeth Warren and Bernie Sanders advocated for a total fracking ban in 2020, the official Democratic Party platform did not include a fracking prohibition, and the party's presidential nominee Joe Biden repeatedly and explicitly stated, "I am not banning fracking." Biden's policy focused on banning new oil and gas drilling on federal lands, not a nationwide ban on all fracking. Fact-checking organizations assessed the claim that Biden's platform called for a total ban as "Mostly false," and during the debate where Pence made the claim, Kamala Harris directly rebutted it.

The evidence is sufficient to render a clear decision. The core of the claim—that "they" (interpreted as the Democratic Party or its 2020 presidential ticket) want to ban fracking—is explicitly contradicted by the party's official platform, the nominee's repeated denials, and the direct rebuttal in the debate context. While some individual Democrats supported a ban, the party's official stance and its standard-bearer did not.

Based on the recorded evidence, the claim that "the Democrats want to ban fracking" is not supported and is directly refuted by the official 2020 party platform and the stated policy of the presidential nominee. Therefore, the decision is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check concludes that the claim is refuted because the official [2020 Democratic Party platform did not include a fracking ban](https://www.spglobal.com/commodityinsights/en/market-insights/latest-news/natural-gas/082520-republicans-warn-of-fracking-ban-oil-and-gas-jobs-loss-on-day-one-of-convention), and presidential nominee [Joe Biden repeatedly stated "I am not banning fracking"](https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291), clarifying his policy would only end new drilling on federal lands. While [some Democratic primary candidates like Bernie Sanders and Elizabeth Warren advocated for a ban](https://www.npr.org/2020/02/11/804487306/proposals-to-ban-fracking-could-hurt-democrats-in-key-states), this was not the party's official position. Furthermore, [Kamala Harris directly rebutted this claim during the vice-presidential debate](https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291), and independent fact-checkers rated similar assertions as ["Mostly false."](https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291)